+## 2026-10-07 — Phase 11a: AI Usage Observability & Cost Control
+
+### D154. Phase 11 split; a local `ai_calls` table is the source of truth, Langfuse an optional exporter (supersedes D8's "via Langfuse alerting")
+**Decision:** Phase 11 (spec §21: "Langfuse, rate limiting, retries, security, indexes, automated testing, E2E tests, cost controls, monitoring, documentation") is split into 11a (AI usage observability + cost control, built here), 11b (structured logging with D9 scrubbing, monitoring), 11c (security, rate limiting, index audit) and 11d (automated E2E in CI, documentation). In 11a every Anthropic and Voyage call writes one row to a new `ai_calls` table (operation, model, tokens, web searches, latency, estimated cost, outcome, short error code — never content). The budget gate sums that table. Langfuse receives the same metadata only when `LANGFUSE_*` is configured.
+**Alternatives considered:** (a) Self-hosted Langfuse in Docker Compose as the only store — Langfuse v3 needs ClickHouse, Redis, S3 and Postgres plus two app containers, and a ceiling that queries it before every call makes all AI depend on an observability tool being up. (b) Langfuse Cloud only — makes a SaaS mandatory (spec §17: "without requiring paid SaaS services"; §23 principle 13). (c) Langfuse alerting as D8 said — alerts do not block anything, so D8's "hard ceiling" was never actually enforceable that way, and nothing enforced it before this phase.
+**Why:** The ceiling must work offline and must not fail open when a third party is down; a local append-only table answers "spent this month" with one indexed SUM. Langfuse remains available for traces and dashboards without being required.
+**What it affects:** `packages/ai/src/usage/`, `packages/db/src/schema/aiCalls.ts`, migrations 0028/0029, `packages/db/src/aiUsage/dbUsageSink.ts`, every AI call site (D157), `apps/web/src/app/usage/`.
+
+### D155. A hard monthly ceiling on estimated spend: gate before every call, fail closed, 429 to the user
+**Decision:** `AI_MONTHLY_BUDGET_USD` (default 20; 0 = no ceiling) and `AI_BUDGET_WARN_PERCENT` (default 80, integer 1–100). A blank value falls back to the default — `z.coerce.number("")` is 0, which would otherwise silently mean "unlimited". The month is the UTC calendar month. Before every tracked call, `checkBudget` sums `estimated_cost_usd` since the month start; at or above the ceiling it records a zero-cost `blocked` row and throws `AiBudgetExceededError` without contacting the provider. If the sum query itself fails, the call is blocked and the error rethrown (fail closed). Web routes answer `429` with the readable message in `error` — the field every AI panel already displays, so no panel changed — plus `code: "ai_budget_exceeded"`, `spentUsd`, `ceilingUsd` and `resetsAt`.
+**Accepted:** Check-then-call is not atomic, so calls already in flight can overshoot the ceiling by their own cost (cents for one user). The ceiling is on estimates (D156), not on the invoice.
+**Alternatives considered:** Blocking background work only (explicit actions would keep spending past the "hard" ceiling). Warning only (leaves D8's promise unenforced). A row lock or advisory lock around check+call (serializes every AI call for a cents-level gain). A per-operation or daily budget (YAGNI).
+**What it affects:** `packages/config/src/env.ts`, `packages/ai/src/usage/budget.ts`, `packages/ai/src/usage/track.ts`, `apps/web/src/lib/aiUsage/budgetResponse.ts`, the 7 AI action routes.
+
+### D156. Cost is estimated from an in-code price table; an unknown model is priced at the most expensive known rate
+**Decision:** `packages/ai/src/usage/prices.ts` lists USD-per-million-token rates by model-id prefix (longest prefix wins; a prefix matches the exact id or the id followed by `-`, so dated ids match their family but `claude-sonnet-50` does not match `claude-sonnet-5`). Cache reads cost 0.1× and cache writes 1.25× the input rate; web search is $10 per 1,000 searches; Voyage `voyage-3.5` is $0.06/MTok. Sources (checked 2026-10-07): Anthropic pricing as cached in the claude-api reference, Anthropic's web-search docs, Voyage's pricing page. Costs are computed in micro-dollars and rounded to whole micro-dollars (the `numeric(12,6)` column). A successful call is priced by the model that served it (`message.model`). An unknown model is priced at that provider's highest known input and output rates and recorded with `price_known = false`, which `/usage` names. A response without a usage block is recorded at zero cost with `price_known = false`.
+**Alternatives considered:** Pricing an unknown model at $0 (a model swap would silently disable the ceiling). Refusing calls to unpriced models (a new model would break every feature until the table is edited). Fetching prices at runtime (no machine-readable source; adds a network dependency to every call).
+**What it affects:** `packages/ai/src/usage/prices.ts`, `packages/ai/src/usage/track.ts`, `apps/web/src/lib/aiUsage/summarizeUsage.ts`.
+
+### D157. One tracked path: a narrow `MessagesClient`, a per-operation `anthropicFor`, a required usage argument on `embedTexts`
+**Decision:** Generators now take `MessagesClient` (non-streaming `messages.create` only) instead of `Pick<Anthropic, "messages">`, so an untracked `stream()`, `countTokens()` or `batches` call is a compile error; a real SDK client is still assignable, so evals and tests are unchanged. Pipelines that make several kinds of call take `anthropicFor: (operation) => MessagesClient` and ask for a client per call (`job_requirements_extraction`, `resume_optimization`, `company_research`, `pitch_generation`, `cover_letter_generation`, `interview_prep_generation`, `match_explanation`); the two single-call routes ask for `resume_extraction` and `career_goal_parse`. `embedTexts(env, texts, { sink, operation })` makes the usage argument required. Every call records one row per logical call (SDK or Voyage retries are not separate rows). `outcome` is transport only — `ok`, `api_error`, `blocked`; schema-validation failures stay with each call site's own `*ValidationError`. `error_code` is a short code (`anthropic:429`, `anthropic:timeout`, `voyage:401`, `network`, `budget_exceeded`, `budget_check_failed`), never a message, because provider messages can echo request content. A failed `record` is logged as `{"event":"ai_usage_record_failed","operation":…,"code":<pg code>}` and never fails a call that already happened. A structural test (`noUntrackedCalls.test.ts`) fails if production code outside `packages/ai` builds an Anthropic client or calls Voyage directly.
+**Alternatives considered:** Inferring the operation from `tool_choice.name` (silently mislabels after a tool rename; company research forces no tool). Wrapping `Pick<Anthropic, "messages">` in a Proxy (still exposes untracked members). An `invalid_output` outcome (would need a hook in all 9 call sites for information each already reports).
+**What it affects:** `packages/ai/src/usage/anthropicFor.ts`, `packages/ai/src/usage/track.ts`, `packages/ai/src/embeddings.ts`, the 9 generators, `runResumeOptimization`, `prepareApplicationContext`, `runPitchGeneration`, `runCoverLetterGeneration`, `runInterviewPrepGeneration`, `runMatching`, `ensureJobRequirements`, `ensureCompanyResearch`, `ensureGoalEmbedding`, `ensureJobEmbeddings`, the web routes and the matching worker.
+
+### D158. Where a budget block surfaces
+**Decision:** User-triggered actions answer 429 (resume upload, goal parse, resume optimization, pitch, research refresh, cover letter, interview prep). Resume upload and goal parse also mark their row failed with `ai_budget_exceeded` and do not retry. `AiBudgetExceededError` is deliberately not an `Anthropic.APIError`, so the pipelines that map `APIError` to a generic 502 or cache a failed research row let it through: a blocked company research is never cached as `failed`. Embedding call sites keep D56's "degrade, never block" rule — a blocked embedding is recorded and degrades to "no embedding" exactly like a Voyage outage (so the profile and goal confirm routes never return 429). Matching stops explaining at the first blocked explanation and still completes the run with every score stored; `ensureJobEmbeddings` stops at the first blocked chunk and counts the remaining jobs as failed — both avoid writing one pointless blocked row per remaining job.
+**Alternatives considered (corrected during planning):** The spec draft said a blocked job/goal embedding would fail the matching job. The existing code deliberately swallows every embedding error (D56), and a budget block is no reason to fail a run whose scores are otherwise complete.
+**What it affects:** the 7 routes, `runMatching`, `ensureJobEmbeddings`, `ensureGoalEmbedding`, `saveConfirmedProfile`, `confirmCareerGoal`, `runResumeOptimization`.
+
+### D159. `ai_calls` is append-only by RLS policy; `DbUsageSink` lives in the db package without importing the AI package
+**Decision:** Migration 0029 enables RLS with a `FOR SELECT` and a `FOR INSERT` policy only, so UPDATE and DELETE by the app role match no rows. `operation` is plain text (validated by the `AiOperation` union) so a new operation needs no migration; `provider`, `outcome` and non-negative counts/cost are CHECKs. Index `(user_id, created_at DESC)`. No FK to users (no other table needs one either). The retention sweep does not touch it: rows hold no content or PII. `DbUsageSink` declares its own structurally identical `AiCallRecord` and implements `{ spendSinceUsd, record }`; `AiUsageSink` declares those as properties (not methods) so TypeScript checks the parameter types contravariantly where web and the worker assign one to the other — any drift is a compile error.
+**Alternatives considered:** Restricting the app role with GRANTs (the default privileges in `infra/postgres/init.sql` and CI grant UPDATE/DELETE on every table; a REVOKE in a migration would be undone by every test suite's re-grant). Making `packages/db` depend on `packages/ai` (pulls the AI SDK into the lowest layer) or `packages/ai` depend on `packages/db`.
+**What it affects:** `packages/db/src/schema/aiCalls.ts`, `packages/db/migrations/0028_*.sql`, `0029_ai_calls_rls.sql`, `packages/db/src/aiUsage/dbUsageSink.ts`, `packages/ai/src/usage/types.ts`.
+
+### D160. Langfuse export as OpenTelemetry spans over HTTP, metadata only (spec §6 revised in planning)
+**Decision:** When `LANGFUSE_HOST`, `LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY` and `LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY` are all set (`loadEnv` rejects a partial set; blank counts as unset), `withLangfuseExport` wraps the sink: after the `ai_calls` row is written it POSTs one OTLP/HTTP JSON span to `{LANGFUSE_HOST}/api/public/otel/v1/traces` with basic auth and `x-langfuse-ingestion-version: 4`. Span ids derive from the row id; attributes set `langfuse.observation.type=generation`, the model, `usage_details`, `cost_details`, level (`DEFAULT`/`WARNING` blocked/`ERROR`) and metadata (provider, outcome, error code, price_known) — never `input`, `output`, prompt or response text. The export is fire-and-forget with a 3 s timeout; failures are counted in a process-local counter shown by `GET /api/health` (`aiUsage.langfuseExportFailures`) and never thrown.
+**Why OTLP, not the batch ingestion API the spec named:** Langfuse's own OpenAPI document marks `POST /api/public/ingestion` deprecated and shut down on Langfuse Cloud on 2026-11-16 for everything except scores; OTLP is the supported write path for v4. JSON OTLP needs no SDK.
+**Alternatives considered:** The Langfuse JS SDK or the OpenTelemetry SDK (a dependency and a background batching process for one span per call). Exporting prompts/responses for richer traces (sends resume and job text to a third party; CLAUDE.md §9).
+**What it affects:** `packages/ai/src/usage/langfuse.ts`, `packages/config/src/env.ts`, `apps/web/src/app/api/health/route.ts`, `apps/web/src/lib/aiUsage/createUsageSink.ts`, `services/matching-worker/src/main.ts`.
+
+### D161. Voyage retry policy, and Voyage error messages carry the status only
+**Decision:** `embedTexts` makes up to 3 attempts, retrying network errors, 429 and 5xx, never other 4xx. The delay is a seconds-valued `retry-after` (capped at 30 s) when present, else 500 ms × 2^(attempt−1) with ±20% jitter. The budget is checked once, before the first attempt; one row records the final outcome and total latency. A non-2xx response throws `VoyageRequestError("Voyage embeddings request failed: <status>")` — the response body is no longer included, because it can echo the embedded text. Anthropic retries are unchanged (the SDK's default `maxRetries: 2`).
+**What it affects:** `packages/ai/src/embeddings.ts`.
+
+### D162. `/usage` page and a home-page badge fetched in the browser
+**Decision:** `GET /api/usage` summarizes this UTC month's rows (spend vs ceiling and state, by feature with blocked/failed counts and tokens, by model over calls that reached a provider, unknown-price models, the last 20 failed or blocked calls with codes). Sums are in micro-dollars. `/usage` renders it with a progress bar, a warn note, an alert when over, and "Costs are estimates from a built-in price table, not your Anthropic or Voyage invoice." The home page links to it; the link is a client component that fetches `/api/usage` and shows "N% of AI budget" (floored) at warn or "AI budget reached" when over, nothing otherwise — the home page is prerendered at build time, so a server-side query would have frozen build-time numbers into it. Feature names come from a map typed over `AI_OPERATIONS`, so a new operation without a label is a compile error. There is no global header, so the badge is on the home page only.
+**What it affects:** `apps/web/src/app/api/usage/route.ts`, `apps/web/src/lib/aiUsage/summarizeUsage.ts`, `apps/web/src/lib/aiUsage/format.ts`, `apps/web/src/app/usage/`, `apps/web/src/app/AiUsageLink.tsx`, `apps/web/src/app/page.tsx`.
+
 *Entries are appended chronologically. Do not edit or delete past entries when a decision is later reversed — add a new entry that supersedes it and cross-reference the original.*
